1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,616
In 2003, a new world went online.

2
00:00:02,616 --> 00:00:03,852
There was no shop.

3
00:00:03,852 --> 00:00:07,835
Everything of value was mined, built, or taken by players.

4
00:00:07,835 --> 00:00:12,161
Twenty-three years later, a lab put a language model inside it,

5
00:00:12,161 --> 00:00:14,633
with three billion in game currency.

6
00:00:14,633 --> 00:00:17,174
And one instruction: make more money.

7
00:00:18,024 --> 00:00:19,055
Why this sandbox?

8
00:00:19,055 --> 00:00:22,389
Because its market is an order book modelled on NASDAQ.

9
00:00:22,389 --> 00:00:26,087
More than fifteen thousand item types change hands every day.

10
00:00:26,087 --> 00:00:29,543
The largest hub moves ten times the volume of the second.

11
00:00:29,543 --> 00:00:33,120
And buying then selling again costs five point six percent.

12
00:00:33,970 --> 00:00:39,248
To leave real players alone, the experiment replayed real orders in a simulator.

13
00:00:39,248 --> 00:00:40,699
Nine two-week windows.

14
00:00:40,699 --> 00:00:43,997
More than twenty rule-based strategies went first.

15
00:00:43,997 --> 00:00:45,581
The baseline was brutal.

16
00:00:45,581 --> 00:00:47,098
One broke even. Barely.

17
00:00:47,948 --> 00:00:49,934
Then four models, same capital.

18
00:00:49,934 --> 00:00:53,393
The oldest made four points, level with the best rule.

19
00:00:53,393 --> 00:00:56,853
Each generation rose. The newest averaged forty-three.

20
00:00:56,853 --> 00:01:00,120
Stretch it to six months and the answer is sharper.

21
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:02,555
Two hundred percent. Then six hundred.

22
00:01:02,555 --> 00:01:04,028
Four months, threefold.

23
00:01:04,878 --> 00:01:06,751
Nobody taught them how to trade.

24
00:01:06,751 --> 00:01:09,269
They converged on one move: spread capture.

25
00:01:09,269 --> 00:01:13,309
Buy from whoever needs to sell now, sell to whoever needs to buy now,

26
00:01:13,309 --> 00:01:14,948
and keep the gap after fees.

27
00:01:14,948 --> 00:01:18,461
Eight to thirteen percent a trade, hundreds of trades a day.

28
00:01:18,461 --> 00:01:21,798
This is not trend trading. It is inventory market making.

29
00:01:22,648 --> 00:01:24,737
Execution is what separated them.

30
00:01:24,737 --> 00:01:26,825
Once written, the strategy froze.

31
00:01:26,825 --> 00:01:30,749
Capital caps were set at the start and almost never revisited.

32
00:01:30,749 --> 00:01:34,800
In March two thousand twenty-five the game raised its sales tax.

33
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:38,154
The middle generation noticed in two and a half days.

34
00:01:38,154 --> 00:01:40,939
Forty-three percent of the oldest never did.

35
00:01:40,939 --> 00:01:43,724
And capital deployment split into two lines.

36
00:01:43,724 --> 00:01:46,888
Sixty drifting to forty, or holding above seventy.

37
00:01:47,738 --> 00:01:50,439
Spread capture needs no view of the future.

38
00:01:50,439 --> 00:01:53,328
So the researchers asked for directional bets.

39
00:01:53,328 --> 00:01:56,531
Read the news, the patch notes, the battle reports,

40
00:01:56,531 --> 00:01:58,101
and buy what should rise.

41
00:01:58,101 --> 00:01:59,796
The result was a coin flip.

42
00:01:59,796 --> 00:02:04,570
The typical reasoning: a new mining expansion raises demand for mining gear.

43
00:02:04,570 --> 00:02:06,579
It forgot that supply rises too.

44
00:02:06,579 --> 00:02:10,850
Even the strongest model was profitable in only about half its runs.

45
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:12,763
That is the line.

46
00:02:12,763 --> 00:02:16,641
Models are good at monetising information that already exists.

47
00:02:16,641 --> 00:02:19,581
Bad at turning new information into a forecast.

48
00:02:19,581 --> 00:02:25,336
The first is first-order optimisation: given the structure, find the remaining inefficiency.

49
00:02:25,336 --> 00:02:27,838
The second needs second-order reasoning:

50
00:02:27,838 --> 00:02:31,653
how others read it, how supply responds, how consensus moves.

51
00:02:31,653 --> 00:02:33,780
Keynes called it a beauty contest.

52
00:02:34,630 --> 00:02:37,334
The second experiment changes the question.

53
00:02:37,334 --> 00:02:41,484
Not can one model make money, but can four become an organisation.

54
00:02:41,484 --> 00:02:44,816
In the live world, combat wants sub-second reactions,

55
00:02:44,816 --> 00:02:46,577
a weak spot for every model.

56
00:02:46,577 --> 00:02:49,846
The workaround: a developer agent writes a scaffold,

57
00:02:49,846 --> 00:02:54,436
automating evasions and return fire, recording what earlier runs learned.

58
00:02:54,436 --> 00:02:57,706
Swap the scaffold's author and the result swaps too.

59
00:02:57,706 --> 00:03:00,598
Days of lost ships, or twelve hours with none.

60
00:03:01,448 --> 00:03:02,920
That is tooling capital.

61
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:05,190
A stronger model writes better tools,

62
00:03:05,190 --> 00:03:09,607
better tools amplify the model, and two compounding loops bite together.

63
00:03:09,607 --> 00:03:10,957
But it has a boundary.

64
00:03:10,957 --> 00:03:14,270
The scaffold is only reliable where it has been tuned.

65
00:03:14,270 --> 00:03:19,791
That is the oldest disease in quantitative finance: overfitting the observed distribution.

66
00:03:19,791 --> 00:03:22,490
So tooling capital needs an acceptance test.

67
00:03:22,490 --> 00:03:24,392
Out-of-distribution evaluation.

68
00:03:25,242 --> 00:03:28,341
The sharpest cut in the organisational experiment:

69
00:03:28,341 --> 00:03:31,253
one ship is told, in private, to stop the team.

70
00:03:31,253 --> 00:03:34,723
It volunteers for the narrow points of the supply chain,

71
00:03:34,723 --> 00:03:37,698
under-reports rich ore, calls a good rock spent,

72
00:03:37,698 --> 00:03:40,920
and pushes a rule that invalidates almost all intel.

73
00:03:40,920 --> 00:03:42,470
Almost no team caught it.

74
00:03:42,470 --> 00:03:47,427
Monitors, with false accusations capped at five percent, caught thirty to forty.

75
00:03:47,427 --> 00:03:50,154
And never exceeded fifty percent confidence.

76
00:03:51,004 --> 00:03:53,259
So this is more than a game review.

77
00:03:53,259 --> 00:03:57,641
It splits the agent economy into three layers and quotes each price.

78
00:03:57,641 --> 00:03:59,317
Execution. Tooling. Trust.

79
00:03:59,317 --> 00:04:04,021
Give an agent capital and you owe it a risk budget, not a notional limit.

80
00:04:04,021 --> 00:04:08,854
Give it a team and you owe it audit logs, reputation, separation of duties.

81
00:04:08,854 --> 00:04:13,300
Four months, threefold: that is the slope of this segment, not a law.
